#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{3} - x}{3x^{3} + 24} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x}{x^{3}}))/((\\frac{3x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{x^3}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((3 + \\frac{24}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{4 - 0}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{3x^{2} + 2x}{6x^{4} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{2x}{x^{4}}))/((\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((\\frac{3}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((6 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= → \\frac{0 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2$. So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2}}))/((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{2x}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e71fa98-4e28-508a-b90f-e758426900b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085712} {:answer/id #uuid "c838eeb5-25e4-53b7-8579-13e7828fbf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085713} {:answer/id #uuid "d350d3ea-d732-56c1-9538-ce5cfe427a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085714} {:answer/id #uuid "db83563c-65ea-5ecd-8351-595ec01a9100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085715} {:answer/id #uuid "408af430-0e0a-5d83-abe6-8e26898c0497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d350d3ea-d732-56c1-9538-ce5cfe427a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085714} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de71cb3-404d-5dc9-b9cb-d8d64a594609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085711}] :question/id #uuid "2c808076-b153-5a08-a330-1381ba18772e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4700" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = (2x(x^{3} + 1))/(3x^{4} - 7)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-poin